5:[["$","$L10","1.11",{"topic":{"id":"1.11","title":"Social Media Ethics Code, 2021 and National IT Policy, 2013","minutes":16,"summary":"Separate platform due diligence, digital-media ethics, and national cybersecurity policy—and understand the 2026 changes.","sections":[{"title":"The three parts of the 2021 Rules","body":["The IT (Intermediary Guidelines and Digital Media Ethics Code) Rules, 2021 contain Part I introductory provisions, Part II intermediary due diligence, and Part III digital-media ethics. Part II includes Rule 3 duties and additional Rule 4 duties for significant social media intermediaries. Part III covers publishers of news and current affairs and publishers of online curated content, with different administrative mechanisms.","Grievance redressal, prescribed notices, and record-retention requirements must be read in the exact rule and version. Rule 3(1)(d) addresses removal following actual knowledge through the specified court or government route. Other complaint categories have their own conditions and clocks. The 2025 amendment tightened authorization and reasoning safeguards and changed the general specified removal window from 36 to 24 hours."]},{"title":"Synthetic information: the 2026 amendment","body":["G.S.R. 120(E), notified on 10 February 2026, amended the 2021 Rules and generally commenced on 20 February 2026, with subsequent corrigenda. It introduced duties concerning synthetically generated audio, visual, or audio-visual information, including declarations, verification, labels, and provenance-related measures within the notified conditions. It also reduced the Rule 3(1)(d) window to three hours. Do not apply that clock to every grievance.","The definition and exclusions matter: not every edit or text-only model output is treated identically. The final text differs from the consultation draft, including proposed numerical label-size requirements. A platform response should inspect the notified Rule 3 and official corrections, not rely on a headline claiming that all AI content must carry a label of a fixed percentage of the screen."]},{"title":"Policy is not the same as a rule","body":["The commonly intended 2013 policy is the National Cyber Security Policy, 2013, not a separate penal “National IT Policy”. Its objectives included a secure cyber ecosystem, workforce development, indigenous security technologies, information sharing, and incident response. It helped frame national priorities but does not itself supply every element of a criminal offence.","Compare policy and regulation through their legal effects. A policy may encourage capacity building; a Rule can impose a due-diligence condition; an Act can authorize a sanction. An organization can align with a policy while still failing a binding obligation. Keep the policy’s date and later strategies or notifications visible when discussing present-day governance."]}],"practice":{"title":"Triage three different complaints","scenario":"A platform receives a valid court removal direction, an impersonation complaint, and a report of a realistic AI-generated video.","steps":["Classify each request under the relevant part and rule.","Check the current removal clock and triggering conditions separately.","For synthetic media, review declaration, verification, and labelling duties and exclusions.","Document the decision, user notice, and preservation needs."],"debrief":"The same platform can face different legal clocks and processes at the same time. One universal “24-hour takedown rule” is not an accurate answer."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["it-2021","policy-2013"],"sources":["it2021","it2026","policy2013","rules"],"labId":"email","unit":1,"objectives":["Explain the three parts of the 2021 Rules and apply it to an investigation.","Explain synthetic information: the 2026 amendment and apply it to an investigation.","Explain policy is not the same as a rule and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-1.11","prompt":"Which statement about the 2013 document is correct?","options":["It is the National Cyber Security Policy and is distinct from binding IT Rules.","It repealed the Evidence Act.","It created the BSA certificate Schedule.","It is identical to the 2021 Ethics Code."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The 2013 policy sets cybersecurity objectives; the later Rules impose specific legal duties under the IT Act.","unit":1}}}]]
